      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP Build Trades 2027: Getting the SkilledTradesBC Credential for a Targeted Invitation
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        A job offer in one of nine construction trades is not enough on its own. To be picked in a Build draw you need a SkilledTradesBC certificate or a registered apprenticeship that matches the job. Here is how each route works, what it costs and how long it takes.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>14 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-orange-600 mb-2">9</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Priority construction trades (NOC codes)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">791</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Build invitations, May to Oct 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">88&ndash;108</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Range of 2026 Build cutoff scores</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        On April 23, 2026, the BC Provincial Nominee Program reorganized its Skills Immigration priorities around three objectives: Care, Build and Innovate. Under Build, the program said it would "prioritize certified workers in 9 key in-demand skilled trades" to support construction and major infrastructure. Since May 2026, those trades have had their own invitation rounds. This guide is about the one requirement that most often stops tradespeople from being selected: the SkilledTradesBC credential. If you want the broader picture of trades immigration, our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-skilled-trades-construction-pathway-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">skilled trades pathway overview</a> covers it; this article focuses on certification.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Exact Rule for a Build Invitation</h2>

      <p>
        WelcomeBC's "selection of workers" list sets out who can receive a targeted Build invitation. The wording matters, so here it is in full: "To receive a targeted invitation to apply, workers in construction trades must have a valid trade certificate issued by, or have a trades apprenticeship registered with, SkilledTradesBC which corresponds with the job they have been offered."
      </p>

      <p>Three practical consequences follow from that sentence:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>A registered apprenticeship counts.</strong> You do not need to be a certified journeyperson. The May 6, 2026 Build draw was described on WelcomeBC as covering "all priority construction occupations (including workers who have apprenticeships registered with SkilledTradesBC)."</li>
        <li><strong>The credential must match the job offer.</strong> A carpenter's certificate does not help a job offer coded as a welder. The trade on your credential and the NOC on your job offer need to line up.</li>
        <li><strong>The credential is on top of normal Skills Immigration rules.</strong> You still apply through the Skilled Worker stream, which requires a full-time, indeterminate job offer from an eligible B.C. employer in a TEER 0 to 3 occupation and at least two years of full-time skilled work experience within the last ten years (Program Guide section 4.1). For TEER 2 jobs, which is where all nine trades sit, you need at least CLB 4 in each language skill (section 3.4).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Key point</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">If you misstate your trade status at registration, the consequences are serious. The Program Guide (section 7.3) says that if you receive a targeted invitation and it is later found that you did not meet the factors used to target you, your application will be refused.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 9 Build Trades and Their BC Certification Programs</h2>

      <p>
        The NOC codes below are the ones WelcomeBC lists under Build. The second column is the SkilledTradesBC program we would normally expect to correspond to each NOC; that mapping is our reading, not an official BC PNP table, so check that your credential's trade name fits your job duties. Apprenticeship and challenge figures are taken from each trade's SkilledTradesBC page and program profile.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">NOC (BC PNP list)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">SkilledTradesBC program</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Apprenticeship (technical / work-based)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Hours to challenge</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Compulsory in BC?</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72106 Welders and related machine operators</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Welder (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">26 weeks over 3 years / 4,620 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,930 + practical assessment</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72200 Electricians (except industrial and power system)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Construction Electrician (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">40 weeks over 4 years / 6,000 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72201 Industrial electricians</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Industrial Electrician (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">40 weeks over 4 years / 6,000 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72300 Plumbers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Plumber (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">30 weeks over 4 years / 6,300 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,450</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3" rowspan="2">72301 Steamfitters, pipefitters and sprinkler system installers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Steamfitter/Pipefitter (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">30 weeks over 4 years / 6,300 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,450</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sprinkler Fitter (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">24 weeks over 3 years / 6,480 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,720</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72310 Carpenters</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Carpenter (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">28 weeks over 4 years / 6,360 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,540</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72400 Construction millwrights and industrial mechanics</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Industrial Mechanic (Millwright) (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">28 weeks over 4 years / 6,360 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,540</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72401 Heavy-duty equipment mechanics</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Heavy Duty Equipment Technician (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">28 weeks over 4 years / 6,330 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,495</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">72402 Heating, refrigeration and air conditioning mechanics</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Refrigeration and Air Conditioning Mechanic (Red Seal)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">33 weeks over 4 years / 6,210 hrs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9,315</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Yes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        "Compulsory" refers to B.C.'s Skilled Trades Certification system. SkilledTradesBC lists seven compulsory trades: Construction Electrician, Industrial Electrician, Powerline Technician, Steamfitter/Pipefitter, Refrigeration and Air Conditioning Mechanic, Gasfitter Class A and B, and Sheet Metal Worker. Four of them overlap with the Build list. In those four, you cannot legally work in B.C. at all unless you are a registered apprentice, a certified journeyperson, or approved as a Trade Qualifier or Uncertified Experienced Worker. Mobile and tower crane operators become compulsory on July 5, 2027, but crane operators are not on the BC PNP Build list.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Route 1: You Already Hold a Canadian Certificate or Red Seal</h2>

      <p>
        If you completed an apprenticeship in another province and hold a Certificate of Qualification or an Interprovincial Red Seal, SkilledTradesBC says you already meet B.C.'s compulsory-trade requirement. For BC PNP registration points, the Program Guide's professional designation table says a valid trade certificate "includes Red Seal endorsement certificates issued by other Canadian provinces and territories."
      </p>

      <p>
        There is one wrinkle. The Build targeting rule on WelcomeBC refers to a certificate "issued by" SkilledTradesBC. The Program Guide accepts out-of-province Red Seals for points, but we could not find a statement that explicitly says the same about targeted Build invitations. If your only credential is from another province, email the BC PNP (PNPInfo@gov.bc.ca) or ask SkilledTradesBC whether you should obtain a B.C. certificate through its labour mobility process before you register.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Route 2: Challenge the Certification Exam as a Trade Qualifier</h2>

      <p>
        This is the main route for experienced tradespeople trained outside Canada, or anyone who learned the trade on the job without an apprenticeship. SkilledTradesBC describes the challenge pathway as being for people with "over five years of practical experience" who want to "become certified without going through an apprenticeship program."
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Step by step</h3>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Find your trade and its declaration form.</strong> Each trade page on SkilledTradesBC has an Employer Declaration and a Statutory Declaration form showing the hours, the percentage of job tasks you must have performed, and any prerequisite credentials.</li>
        <li><strong>Count your documented hours.</strong> The challenge thresholds in the table above are roughly one and a half times the apprenticeship work-based hours. Hours must be "directly related experience working in the occupation" and you must have the required scope of tasks, not just the total.</li>
        <li><strong>Gather proof.</strong> Submit one Employer Declaration (or a Statutory Declaration if you were self-employed or cannot reach a supervisor) for each period of work. All documents must be in English, so foreign employer letters need translation.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare your references.</strong> SkilledTradesBC phones references and also calls the companies to confirm those references worked there. If references cannot be contacted, the application can be denied. This is the step where foreign experience most often stalls.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply online and pay $120.</strong> Applications go through the SkilledTradesBC Portal.</li>
        <li><strong>Wait for assessment.</strong> SkilledTradesBC's pages give different estimates: about 6 to 8 weeks on its compulsory-trades page, 8 to 10 weeks on its newcomers page, and 10 to 12 weeks on its general challenge page, in each case longer if your experience is from outside Canada. Plan for at least three months.</li>
        <li><strong>Write the exam within 24 months.</strong> Once approved you have 24 months to schedule and pass. The pass mark is 70 per cent and you get a maximum of four attempts. Welders must also pass a SkilledTradesBC standardized practical assessment after the written exam.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Language is less of a barrier than many expect. SkilledTradesBC says you do not need IELTS or CELPIP results to write its exams, and you can request accommodations such as a translation dictionary or a translator. You will still need CLB 4 or higher for the BC PNP itself.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">Compulsory trades: approval is not the same as certification</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">In the four compulsory Build trades, an approved Trade Qualifier receives a Temporary Authorization that lets them keep working for 24 months while preparing for the exam. The Build targeting rule names only a certificate or a registered apprenticeship. It does not mention Trade Qualifier status, so do not assume a Temporary Authorization alone will get you a Build invitation. Pass the exam first, or register as an apprentice if that fits your situation.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Route 3: Register as an Apprentice</h2>

      <p>
        If you are already working in B.C. in one of the trades but do not have five years of experience, a registered apprenticeship may be the faster way to qualify for Build targeting. According to SkilledTradesBC, you need an employer or sponsor willing to register you, you must meet any registration prerequisites for the trade, and you must register with SkilledTradesBC. To work as an apprentice you must be legally authorized to work in Canada. To attend technical training you must be legally authorized to study in Canada and meet the training provider's requirements, which can be an issue on some work permits, so check before enrolling.
      </p>

      <p>
        Workers with one to four years of practical experience can also challenge level exams to enter the apprenticeship at a higher level. SkilledTradesBC says there is no fee to challenge a level exam or to rewrite it.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you are nominated as an apprentice, the Program Guide adds a condition of nomination: you "must continue to make active and ongoing progress towards obtaining your trade certification" until you become a permanent resident.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Costs and Timelines at a Glance</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Item</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Amount or time</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Source</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Challenge application (covers the 1st exam attempt)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$120</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Certification exam, 2nd attempt</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No fee; 30-day wait</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Certification exam, 3rd and 4th attempt (maximum for Trade Qualifiers)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$100 each; advisor approval before the 3rd, proof of upgrading before the 4th</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Level exam challenge</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No fee</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Challenge assessment</td><td class="px-4 py-3">About 6 to 12 weeks depending on the page; longer for foreign experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Window to pass after approval</td><td class="px-4 py-3">24 months</td><td class="px-4 py-3">SkilledTradesBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">BC PNP registration</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Free</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Program Guide 1.8</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">BC PNP Skills Immigration application</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$1,750 (since January 22, 2026)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">WelcomeBC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Time to submit after an invitation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">30 calendar days, cannot be extended</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Program Guide 7.3</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Exam preparation courses, translations of foreign documents and travel to one of the exam locations are extra and vary by person, so we have not put numbers on them. For federal permanent residence fees and other costs, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-cost-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP cost guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How Your Trade Credential Affects Your Score</h2>

      <p>
        Build draws are still ranked by your Skills Immigration registration score out of 200. The credential gives you <strong>5 points</strong> under "Eligible professional designation in B.C.", which applies to trades workers with a valid SkilledTradesBC certificate or registered apprentices. Everything else comes from the standard factors: directly related experience (up to 40), education (up to 40, including the 5 designation points), language (up to 40), hourly wage (up to 55) and area of employment (up to 25). You can estimate yours with our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Worked example A: certified carpenter in Kelowna</h3>
      <p>
        Hypothetical: 6 years as a carpenter, the last 18 months full-time with the same Kelowna employer that made the job offer; foreign trades diploma; B.C. Red Seal certificate obtained by challenge; CLB 6; offered $38.00 an hour.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Experience: 20 (5+ years) + 10 (1 year in Canada) + 10 (currently working for the employer) = 40</li>
        <li>Education: 5 (post-secondary trades certificate) + 5 (professional designation) = 10</li>
        <li>Language: CLB 6 = 15</li>
        <li>Wage: $38.00 to $38.99 = 23</li>
        <li>Area: Area 3 = 15</li>
        <li><strong>Total: 103</strong>, above every 2026 Build cutoff listed below.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Worked example B: apprentice electrician in Surrey</h3>
      <p>
        Hypothetical: 3 years of experience, currently working full-time for the sponsoring employer and registered as an apprentice; high school only; CLB 5; offered $32.00 an hour.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Experience: 12 + 10 + 10 = 32</li>
        <li>Education: 0 + 5 (registered apprentice) = 5</li>
        <li>Language: CLB 5 = 10</li>
        <li>Wage: $32.00 to $32.99 = 17</li>
        <li>Area: Metro Vancouver = 0</li>
        <li><strong>Total: 64</strong>, well below the 2026 range. The fastest gains would be language (CLB 7 adds 10 points over CLB 5) and, over time, the wage increases that come with apprenticeship levels.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Build Draws in 2026</h2>

      <p>Every Build round published on WelcomeBC's invitations page so far:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Minimum score</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Invitations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">May 6, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">108</td><td class="px-6 py-3">121</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">June 2, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">101</td><td class="px-6 py-3">128</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">July 9, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">97</td><td class="px-6 py-3">136</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">August 6, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">88</td><td class="px-6 py-3">187</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">September 10, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">94</td><td class="px-6 py-3">104</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">October 8, 2026</td><td class="px-6 py-3">94</td><td class="px-6 py-3">115</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two patterns stand out. Build rounds have run roughly monthly, alongside the Care rounds. And the cutoff fell from 108 in May to the high 80s and 90s by late summer. That is consistent with a backlog of high-scoring certified tradespeople being invited first, after which the cutoff settles where new registrations come in. For context, the April 23 announcement said 826 construction and trades workers had been nominated since 2022 "after targeting select certified trades."
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to Expect in 2027 (Analysis)</h2>

      <p>
        Nothing about 2027 Build draws has been announced. BC's 2026 allocation rose to 6,254 nominations after a federal top-up on August 18, 2026, and the BC PNP said it plans to use it all by December 31, 2026. The 2027 allocation will not be known until after the federal 2027 to 2029 Levels Plan is released, which we expect in early November 2026. With that caveat, our expectations are:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Build is likely to remain a standing category.</strong> It is one of the three objectives the program built its 2026 guide around, so we would be surprised if it disappeared in 2027.</li>
        <li><strong>Cutoffs will track the allocation.</strong> If 2027 nominations are similar to or lower than 2026, we would expect Build cutoffs to stay in the same general band or drift up. A larger allocation would give more room. We are not predicting a specific number.</li>
        <li><strong>Certification lead times are the real bottleneck.</strong> A challenge started in early 2027 may not produce a certificate until mid-2027 or later once assessment and exam booking are counted. If you plan to be in the 2027 pool, start the SkilledTradesBC process now.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Federal Backup Routes for Certified Trades</h2>

      <p>
        A B.C. certificate also helps outside the BC PNP. The Federal Skilled Trades Program requires either a qualifying job offer or "a certificate of qualification" issued by a provincial or territorial authority, plus two years of full-time trade experience within the last five years. Express Entry's trade occupations category, which lists all nine Build NOCs, requires at least 12 months of full-time work experience in one listed occupation within the past three years. IRCC's rounds data shows two trade occupations draws in 2026: April 2 (3,000 invitations, lowest CRS 477) and October 1 (3,500 invitations, lowest CRS 476). If you are in Express Entry, choosing the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry BC option</a> when you register lets a BC nomination add to your federal profile.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Who This Route Suits, and Who It Does Not</h2>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
        <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <p class="font-bold text-green-900 mb-3">Good fit</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-green-900 text-base">
            <li>Journeypersons with a B.C. certificate and a B.C. job offer in the same trade</li>
            <li>Foreign-trained tradespeople with five-plus years of documented, verifiable experience</li>
            <li>Workers already employed in B.C. whose employer will register them as apprentices</li>
            <li>Anyone willing to work outside Metro Vancouver, where area points add up to 25</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <p class="font-bold text-red-900 mb-3">Poor fit</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-red-900 text-base">
            <li>Construction labourers, helpers and other trades not on the nine-NOC list</li>
            <li>Workers whose experience cannot be verified by phone with former employers</li>
            <li>Candidates without two years of skilled work experience in the last ten years</li>
            <li>Workers whose employer cannot meet BC PNP employer rules, for example placement agencies, which are ineligible</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Registering before the credential exists.</strong> Targeting is based on your registration. Wait until your certificate is issued or your apprenticeship is registered.</li>
        <li><strong>Trade and NOC mismatch.</strong> An HVAC job offer coded as NOC 72402 with a Sheet Metal Worker credential does not "correspond." Match the job duties, the NOC and the credential.</li>
        <li><strong>Counting hours loosely.</strong> SkilledTradesBC checks both total hours and the scope of tasks. A worker who mostly did framing may struggle to show the full carpenter task profile.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming Temporary Authorization equals certification.</strong> See the warning above.</li>
        <li><strong>Inflating the wage.</strong> The guide says applications will not be approved if the wage appears inflated to raise a score, and only the base hourly rate counts, not overtime or premiums.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-3 not-prose">
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Do I need a SkilledTradesBC certificate to get a BC PNP Build invitation?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">WelcomeBC says that to receive a targeted Build invitation, workers in the nine construction trades must have a valid trade certificate issued by SkilledTradesBC, or a trades apprenticeship registered with SkilledTradesBC, that corresponds with the job they have been offered. A registered apprenticeship is therefore an alternative to a full certificate.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">How many hours do I need to challenge a trade certification exam in BC?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">It depends on the trade. SkilledTradesBC program profiles list, for example, 9,000 documented hours for Construction Electrician and Industrial Electrician, 9,450 for Plumber and Steamfitter/Pipefitter, 9,540 for Carpenter and Industrial Mechanic (Millwright), 9,495 for Heavy Duty Equipment Technician, 9,315 for Refrigeration and Air Conditioning Mechanic, 9,720 for Sprinkler Fitter and 6,930 for Welder.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">How much does it cost to challenge a trade in BC?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">SkilledTradesBC charges a $120 fee to apply to challenge a certification exam, which covers the first attempt. A second attempt has no fee; a third and fourth attempt cost $100 each, with a 30-day wait between attempts. Trade Qualifiers get a maximum of four attempts and need 70 per cent to pass.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Does a Red Seal from another province count for BC PNP?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">For registration points, the Skills Immigration Program Guide says a valid trade certificate includes Red Seal endorsement certificates issued by other Canadian provinces and territories. The Build targeted-invitation rule on WelcomeBC refers to a certificate issued by SkilledTradesBC, so confirm with the BC PNP before relying on an out-of-province credential for a Build invitation.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">What scores did BC PNP Build draws need in 2026?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">WelcomeBC lists six Build: Construction Trades draws from May to October 2026 with minimum scores of 108, 101, 97, 88, 94 and 94, issuing 791 invitations in total. Cutoffs for 2027 have not been announced and will depend on BC's 2027 allocation.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

